      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222207/assets/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta?$",
      "worked_solution": "We're given that $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ.\n$$",
